  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1015 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nSaturday through Wednesday:\n\nSynopsis: Upper troughing will gradually progress into and across \nthe western Great Lakes Saturday and Saturday night. An affiliated \ncold front will move into the Upper Mississippi Valley by Saturday \nafternoon, steadily advancing across southern Wisconsin Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning. Rain and perhaps a few rumbles of thunder \nare expected along the passing front Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning, with conditions drying out by Sunday afternoon. Canadian \nair will pour into the area behind the departing front Sunday \nevening, translating to much colder conditions Sunday night into the \nmiddle portions of next week. Broad, post-frontal high pressure will \nbe the predominant synoptic feature regionally Monday through at \nleast Wednesday, leading to minimal precipitation chances and chilly \novernight periods. \n\nSaturday night into Sunday morning: Anticipating a window of \nwidespread rain potential as a cold front & affiliated area of upper \ndivergence move across southern Wisconsin. Have maintained precip \nprobabilities from the NBM, whose peak values (~70+%) peak through \nthe period of anticipated peak forcing. Forecast soundings do hint \nat some weak (~100 J/kg or less) instability in the mid-levels, so \ncan't rule out a few rumbles of thunder as rain moves through. \nSevere hazards are not anticipated in any embedded thunder. \nPrecipitable water values at or slightly above 1\" could lead to some \nheavier downpours in rain showers, though flooding potential will be \nlow given dry antecedent conditions and progressive frontal motion. \nCurrent trends point toward beneficial accumulation in rainfall, \nwith probabilities for >0.25\" in the 50-70+% range at this time.  \n\nSunday night through Wednesday: With high pressure building and \nsteadily progressing from the Northern Plains through the western \nGreat Lakes, overnight periods will be marked by light winds, mostly \nclear skies, and efficient radiational cooling. With Canadian air \nfirmly entrenched, this will translate to widespread lows in the 20s \nareawide Sunday, Monday, and Tuesday nights. Currently appears that \nMonday night could be the coldest of the overnight periods, when \nconservative probabilities from the LREF depict 20-25% chances of \nlows in the 10s across the northwest half of the area. 10s \nprobabilities from the NBM are notably more bullish (~50-70%), \nthough potentially inflated from markedly colder CMC inputs. Whether \nthese colder solutions verify remains uncertain, with trends being \nmonitored into next week. Regardless of where exactly low \ntemperatures end up, winter jackets will be a must early next\nweek. \n\nQuigley",
